<urn:atm:triage:finding/PORT-004-AL8>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "PORT-004-AL8" ;
  triage:title "unix-gating (PORT-004) lint fails on ungated std::os::unix imports in unix_socket.rs" ;
  triage:description "quality-mgr's own 'just lint' run during AL8-QA-2 found the PORT-004 unix-gating lint FAILS on crates/atm-http-runtime/src/unix_socket.rs lines 28, 88, and 111: 'use std::os::unix::...' imports are ungated in production code. Root cause confirmed via git history: pre-extraction (commit ace038f3^ in lib.rs), validate_unix_socket_parent/bind_prepared_unix_socket/verify_prepared_unix_socket each carried their own #[cfg(unix)] attribute directly. The ARCH-001-AL8 extraction (commit ace038f3, 'fix(AL8-RULE-003): split Unix socket adapter') moved them into unix_socket.rs but dropped the per-item #[cfg(unix)] gates, relying only on the module-level '#[cfg(unix)] mod unix_socket;' declaration in lib.rs -- which the PORT-004 lint tool does not treat as sufficient (it appears to lint file-by-file, not cross-file). This is a genuine regression introduced by the ARCH-001-AL8 fix commit itself, not a pre-existing or carried-forward finding." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-al" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "portability-lint" ;
  triage:severity "blocking" ;
  triage:repeatable true ;
  triage:sweepScope "file" ;
  triage:status "fixed" ;
  triage:dispatchReady true ;
  triage:closedAt "2026-08-07T09:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:closedBy "quality-mgr" ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-08-07T06:35:15Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
